A one-time lookup table, built from the constant `mesgs` array on the first call, replaces the linear scan for message numbers below 1024. That should cover most messages in practice.

- **Same results:** the table records the first index for each number, so it returns the same entry the original loop would.
- **Fallback:** numbers of 1024 and above, and any number not in the table, still use the original linear search.
- **Thread safety:** the table is a function-local static. C++ initializes it safely across threads, it depends only on constant profile data, and it is never modified after that.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Table
    {
        short idx[1024];
        Table()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 1024; k++)
                idx[k] = -1;
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (n < 1024 && idx[n] < 0)
                    idx[n] = (short)i;
            }
        }
    };
    static const Table table;

    if (num < 1024)
    {
        int i = table.idx[num];
        if (i >= 0)
            return &mesgs[i];
        return NULL;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```